-- Rifts World Book 33: Northern Gun One, four robots (printed 141-154): Hunter Mobile Gun Original and Block IV, Big Papa, Junior
-- 4 vehicle rows, with 74 locations and 23 weapon entries.
--
-- One-off data script, run once per environment. NOT a migration - it adds
-- rows, not schema.
--
--   node scripts/d1-apply.mjs --local apps/character-creator/db/~288-northern-gun-1-robots-d.sql
--
-- Written by scripts/vessel-sql.mjs from 1 worker file(s). Every value
-- is printable ASCII, every row cites its book, and a Main Body location
-- agrees with its vehicle's mdc_main_body.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO vehicles (slug, name, system, vehicle_class, crew, passengers, speed_ground, speed_air, speed_water, dimensions, weight_tons, mdc_main_body, is_mega_damage, ar, cost, cost_note, description, source_book) VALUES
  ('ng-v7-hunter-original', 'Hunter Mobile Gun NG-V7 "Original"', 'rifts', 'robot', 'Three: a pilot, a copilot and a gunner; the pilot can run every function and weapon alone if he must', 'Four, cramped', 'Running 70 mph (112 km), sustained without tiring the pilot; leaping not recommended: only with a running start, 6 ft (1.8 m) high and 20 ft (6.1 m) across, and on a percentile roll of 01-62 it falls and loses all its attacks for one melee round', 'Not possible', 'Cannot swim; walks the bottom at about half speed; maximum depth 2,500 ft (762 m)', 'Height 32 ft (9.7 m); width 19 ft (5.8 m); length 14 ft (4.3 m)', '34, fully loaded', 490, 1, NULL, 40000000, '40 million credits new. Excellent availability in Michigan, the Midwest, Iron Heart, Free Quebec and much of the Domain of Man; fair across the rest of North America; poor in South America and beyond. Used units: about 2D6% less M.D.C. and 20-40% cheaper. Patchwork rebuilds: 1D4x10% less M.D.C. at every location, 1D4x10% less speed, 1D4 standard features missing or dead and likely one secondary weapon gone, for 40-60% less.', 'Model Type: NG-V7 Block I, II and III (Block III carried 10% more M.D.C. on its limbs, head/turret and main body). Class: Infantry Assault Robot Vehicle. One of Northern Gun''s best-known machines, sold for close to thirty years and built around a massive Howitzer rail gun whose design was recovered late in the Two Hundred Years Dark Age. When it appeared, hardly anyone outside the Coalition and a few nation-states fielded a thirty-foot war machine or a self-propelled field gun, and this was both. Easy to pilot and dependable, it still serves Robot Pilots, mercenaries, explorers and bandits across the Americas, and its humanoid frame and hands let it collect specimens, carry and set up gear, work on other robots and grapple dinosaurs and giants; it fills border patrol, sentry, artillery, troop support, exploration and monster hunting roles. NG kept the design nearly unchanged for generations because buyers wanted it that way, which built a large secondary market of used and rebuilt units (see cost_note). Turret sensor eyes: losing all four kills the main radar, targeting system and optics, leaving the pilot on his own senses with no sensor bonuses to strike, parry or dodge. Behind the main hatch sits an inner one of just 20 M.D.C. Robot P.S. 40. Cargo: minimal, about 4 ft (1.2 m) of space. Power: nuclear, 20 year average life. Hand to hand per Heavy Ground Robot Training (Rifts Ultimate Edition p.352): punches 1D4 M.D. restrained or 2D6 M.D. at full strength; head/turret butt 1D6 M.D.; stomp 2D4 M.D. against targets under 13 ft (4 m) tall; no power punch, leaps or kicks. Sensors beyond the NG standard: four recessed sensors in the head turret, a chest spotlight by the right shoulder swiveling 180 degrees side to side and through a 90 degree vertical arc (2,000 ft/610 m), and four old-style cooling vent tubes on the back, two up and two down.', 'Rifts World Book 33: Northern Gun One p.141-143'),
  ('ng-v7-hunter-block-iv', 'Hunter Mobile Gun NG-V7, Block IV (Upgrade)', 'rifts', 'robot', 'Three: a pilot, a copilot on communications and sensors, and a gunner; the pilot can run every function alone, but it fights best with the weapons split between pilot and gunner', 'Two, cramped', 'Running 70 mph (112 km), sustained without tiring the pilot; leaping not recommended: 7 ft (2.1 m) high and 15 ft (4.6 m) across, doubled with a running start, and on a percentile roll of 01-25 per leap it falls on its face and loses 1D4 melee attacks', 'Not possible', 'Cannot swim; walks the bottom at about half speed; maximum depth 2,500 ft (762 m)', 'Height 32 ft (9.7 m); width 18 ft (5.5 m); length 13 ft (4 m)', '38, fully loaded', 600, 1, NULL, 45000000, 'Nuclear 56 million credits, Solid Oxide Fuel Cell 49 million, electric battery 45 million; the lowest is stored.', 'Model Type: NG-V7, Block IV. Class: Infantry Assault Robot Vehicle. Unveiled in 108 P.A., the biggest revision the Hunter has had: the same frame, design and core weapons as Blocks I-III, so it looks like the old Hunter at a glance, but with reshaped armor carrying more M.D.C., larger feet of a new design, modular weapon options, a light chest gun, better optics and a backup sensor cluster that closes the old blind spots above and below. Crews say it handles exactly like the older blocks. The pilot usually runs the small turrets and the mini-missiles, and the gunner the main turret and sometimes the belly gun. Design team: Charles Walton, Matthew Clements and Sam Beedah. Sensors: losing the four turret eyes no longer matters much; losing the main sensor drum costs -5% on Sensory Equipment, cuts tracked targets from 96 to 48 and radar range by 20%; every sensor must go before radar and targeting fail (then no bonuses to strike, parry or dodge). The inner hatch behind the main hatch has 20 M.D.C. The chest spotlight is wrecked once the main body has taken 450 damage. Robot P.S. 40. Cargo: minimal, about 4 ft (1.2 m) of space. Power: nuclear, 20 year average life; fuel cell and battery models too. Hand to hand per Heavy Ground Robot Training (Rifts Ultimate Edition p.352), Robot P.S. 40. Sensors beyond the NG standard: enhanced radar through the main sensor drum (96 targets, 48 without it), four recessed sensors in the head turret plus a backup sensor suite in the chest, a chest spotlight by the right shoulder swiveling 180 degrees side to side and through a 90 degree vertical arc (1,500 ft/457 m), and four old-style cooling vent tubes on the back, two up and two down.', 'Rifts World Book 33: Northern Gun One p.143-146'),
  ('ng-mmb01-big-papa', 'Mobile Medical Robot NG-MMB-01 "Big Papa"', 'rifts', 'robot', 'Three in the top forward cockpit: the Robot Pilot, a copilot/communications specialist and a Body Doc or other medical advisor (or an Operator, engineer or rescue and excavation specialist); the arms are worked by the pilot or copilot. Onboard medical team of 6-12: ideally 2-4 doctors and 6-8 assistants, often with an Operator or mechanical engineer among them', '18 patients in automated med-beds, and three times as many sitting, lying or standing; printed as 16 beds in the opening description and 18 in the stat block and the features list', 'On treads (vehicle mode) 55 mph (88 km); walking on its legs 20 mph (32 km) maximum; leaping not possible', 'Not possible', 'Fair to poor: walks the bottom at 10 mph (16 km); maximum depth 1,200 ft (366 m)', 'Height 20 ft (6.1 m) on treads with the legs stowed, up to 66 ft (20 m) standing, 86 ft (26.2 m) with the front tilted up at full leg extension; width 28 ft (8.5 m); body length 72 ft (22 m); main arms reach 45 ft (13.7 m), the small folding arms 40 ft (12.2 m)', '25', 580, 1, NULL, 132000000, '132 million credits new, fully fitted with medical gear. Nuclear only. High demand, poor availability; buyers wait months.', 'Model Type: NG MMB-01. Nickname: Big Papa. Class: Mobile Medical Field Unit & Rescue. A big, crewed, all-terrain rescue robot that works as a small mobile hospital, from the Gleba design team (Carl Gleba, Matthew Wood, Christopher Wilmer, Jeremy D. Helton, Benjamin Rodriguez and Sam Beedah). Conceived to pull pilots out of wrecked robots, it grew into a general first responder and is one of NG''s hottest sellers; NG holds a monopoly on medical robots and will paint one to a buyer''s colors. With magic, psychic healing or medical nano-bots, about two-thirds of injuries are handled on the spot and the patient is back in action in 10-30 minutes; field diagnosis is 96% accurate. It rolls on treads for speed and a smooth ride during procedures, and unfolds two big legs to climb debris, stairs and slopes, wade, and rise to reach crews in standing wrecks or people on upper floors and low roofs (66 ft/20 m level, 20 ft/6.1 m more tilted, and the arms reach 45 ft/13.7 m higher still). The look recalls the EX-5 Behemoth Explorer. Four sections: a top-floor medical bay with a roof hatch for airlift, rear lower crew quarters with a small kitchen, a shared shower and three toilets, the forward cockpit behind the spotlights, and a supply hold under the cockpit that can carry wounded if dumped. Built for trauma (cuts, burns, wounds, fractures, surgery), not disease, forensics or lab work. Lightly armed and meant to work under escort; some city or rural units drop the weapons, add water tanks and double as fire engines. The two telescoping hydraulic arms turn 360 degrees at shoulder and elbow; their fingers are rescue tools, each with a camera and light (200 ft/61 m), a bio-scanner and nano-bot release; feeds go to the crew and to any base or hospital within 100 miles (160 km). RMDR-NM12 Remote Medical Delivery Robots, mouse-sized spider-legged runners, carry nano-bots to patients the arms cannot reach. Clamp Finger (one per hand): a two-finger grip that clamps, tears (Robotic P.S. 40), holds or lifts objects or a patient, folding behind the palm. All hatches open by hand. Robot P.S. 40, 50 for the Jaws of Life. Cargo: a 20 ft (6.1 m) chest hold with a 160 ton limit, usually medical gear, folding tables, field equipment and 1D4 portable generators. Power: NGV8 nuclear core, 12 years; no alternatives. Hand to hand per Heavy Ground Robot Training (Rifts Ultimate Edition p.352) at Robot P.S. 40, with attacks and bonuses halved, rounded down: it is not a fighter. Features beyond the NG standard: right folding Sensor Arm (extends 40 ft/12.2 m, turns 360 degrees at shoulder and screen) with an acoustic array like the bionic Ultra-Ear, a thermal imager that sees through smoke, dust, S.D.C. walls, M.D.C. material an inch thick or less and loose earth, a video camera, laser distancer and a flip-down screen with mic and speakers for talking trapped victims through a rescue (arm 30 M.D.C., acoustic array 12, thermal wand 8, monitor 6, all Called Shot at -5); left Multi-Purpose Searchlight Arm under the nose spotlights (extends 40 ft/12.2 m) with camera, distancer, secondary sensors, a 3,000 ft (914 m) light and a 500 mile (805 km) scrambled relay radio (arm 30 M.D.C., sensor box 35); thermal imager 1,800 ft (548.6 m); finger medical scanners reading vitals by touch or within 3 ft (0.9 m), with a 2,000 ft (610 m) distancer and x10 cameras, for +10% to assess injuries; a field clinic of side-compartment awnings with netting, an 18 by 8 ft shelter (printed 5.5 by 5.5 m), plus folding surgical tables and power outlets; 18 automated med-beds stacked three high that hold, medicate, transfuse, feed, oxygenate and monitor; and at least 36 of each nano-robot medical system: IRMSS internal surgeon (75% surgery, 48 robots for four uses, 42,000 credits a kit), IROU Breather (lung work, 50,000), IRVT Seekers (artery and vein survey, 80,000 each, disposable), RAU Cleaners (wound cleaning, 50,000 a pair), RMK Knitter (90% suturing, 24,000) and RSU Sleepers (sedation, 100,000 per set of four).', 'Rifts World Book 33: Northern Gun One p.147-152'),
  ('ng-mmb02-junior', 'Mini-Mobile Medical Robot NG-MMB-02 "Junior"', 'rifts', 'robot', 'Three in the top forward cockpit: the Robot Pilot, a copilot/communications specialist and a Body Doc or other medical advisor (or an engineer or rescue and excavation specialist); the arms are worked by the pilot or copilot. Onboard medical team of four or five: 1-2 doctors and 2-3 assistants, often with an Operator or mechanical engineer among them', '8 patients in automated med-beds, and three times as many sitting, lying or standing', 'On treads (vehicle mode) 45 mph (72 km); walking on its legs 12 mph (19.2 km) maximum; leaping not possible', 'Not possible', 'Fair to poor: walks the bottom at 8 mph (13 km); maximum depth 1,000 ft (305 m)', 'Height 13.6 ft (4.1 m) on treads with the legs stowed, up to 35.6 ft (11 m) standing, 47 ft (14.3 m) with the front tilted up at full leg extension; width 15 ft (4.6 m); body length 29 ft (8.8 m); main arms reach 20 ft (6.1 m), the small folding arms 15 ft (4.6 m)', '15', 445, 1, NULL, 60000000, '70 million credits nuclear with full medical gear, 64 million Solid Oxide Fuel Cell, 60 million electric battery; the lowest is stored. High demand, poor availability.', 'Model Type: NG Mini-MMB-02. Nickname: Junior. Class: Mobile Medical Field Unit & Rescue. A scaled-down Big Papa, alike in look and job but smaller, with fewer beds and leaner resources; inside are two exam rooms that double as operating theaters, a small dispensary and an eight-bed sick bay, with medical stores under the floor. Clamp Finger (one per hand): a two-finger grip that clamps, tears (Robotic P.S. 30), holds or lifts objects or a patient, folding behind the palm. Each finger folds away and has 12 M.D.C. by the weapons text (the location block prints 15); hitting one takes a Called Shot at -5. All hatches open by hand. Robot P.S. 30, 40 for the Jaws of Life. Cargo: a 10 ft (3 m) chest hold with a 35 ton limit, usually medical gear, folding tables, field equipment and 1D4 portable generators. Power: NGV8 nuclear core, 15 years; alternative power supplies can be fitted. Hand to hand per Heavy Ground Robot Training (Rifts Ultimate Edition p.352) at Robot P.S. 30, bonuses halved, rounded down: it is not a fighter. Features beyond the NG standard, as Big Papa''s but smaller: right Sensor Arm (extends 15 ft/4.6 m, turns 360 degrees at shoulder and monitor; arm 20 M.D.C., acoustic array 8, thermal wand 6, monitor 3, all Called Shot at -5); left Multi-Purpose Searchlight Arm (extends 15 ft/4.6 m; arm 20 M.D.C., sensor box 20) with a 2,000 ft (610 m) light and a 500 mile (800 km) relay radio; thermal imager 1,200 ft (366 m); no finger medical scan system; a field clinic awning 10 by 10 ft (3 by 3 m); 8 automated med-beds; and at least 24 of each of Big Papa''s nano-robot medical systems.', 'Rifts World Book 33: Northern Gun One p.152-154');

